  "inputs": {
    "manifest": {
      "specVersion": "1",
      "id": "lax-251941",
      "leanVersion": "v4.30.0",
      "mathlibVersion": "c5ea00351c28e24afc9f0f84379aa41082b1188f",
      "title": "Undecidability of the Post Correspondence Problem",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Mikołaj Bojańczyk"
        },
        {
          "name": "Aristotle (Harmonic)"
        }
      ],
      "bibEntries": []
    },
    "abstract": "The Post correspondence problem is undecidable: no computable function decides, given a finite list of pairs of strings over the natural numbers, whether some nonempty sequence of indices makes the two concatenations equal. The proof follows Sipser's *Introduction to the Theory of Computation*: the acceptance problem for machines is undecidable by diagonalisation (Theorem 4.11, over mathlib's partial recursive programs), every partial recursive function is simulated by a single-tape Turing machine, and a decision procedure for the Post correspondence problem would decide acceptance through the computation-history reduction (Theorem 5.15). This is the result that the book *Transducers* (M. Bojańczyk) takes as given for the undecidability of equivalence of rational relations.\n"
  },
